; Call by reference examples
; =====================================================================

; 1. using default functor
; default functor is always passed by reference (automatically)
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

(set x:x 10)

(func (f p)
  (set p 5))

(f x:x)
(println x:x)   ;-> 5


; 2. using eval
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; Symbol is passed explicitly and dereferenced manually.
; Parameter name uses _p suffix to avoid confusion with global symbol.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

(set y 10)

(func (f y_p)
  (set (eval y_p) 5))

(f 'y)
(println y)     ;-> 5


; 3. using macro
; nice, clean way
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; Runtime macro receives unevaluated argument (symbol),
; performs the write directly.
; Still pure runtime call-by-reference.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

(set z 10)

(mac (f _z)
  (set (eval _z) 5))

(f z)
(println z)     ;-> 5


; 4. using symbol as pointer (eval-based)
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; This example demonstrates TWO things at the same time:
;
; 1) Call-by-reference via symbol pointer
; 2) Why name conflicts do NOT matter
;
; Step by step:
;
; - Global symbol p holds a SYMBOL, not a value.
;   That symbol points to x:x.
;
; - The function parameter is ALSO named p.
;   This shadows the global p, but that is irrelevant.
;
; - What matters is the VALUE of the parameter p,
;   which is the symbol 'x:x.
;
; - eval works on the VALUE (symbol), not on the variable name.
;
; Therefore:
; - local p and global p do not conflict
; - dereferencing is explicit and intentional
; - set writes through the pointer
;
; This is low-level, mechanical, but 100% explicit.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

(set x:x 10)
(set p 'x:x)            ; p holds a symbol pointing to x:x

(func (f p)
  (set (eval p) 35))    ; explicit dereference + write

(f p)
(println x:x)           ;-> 35


; 5. using tie (symbol alias / pointer write)
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; This is the same idea as example 4, but cleaner.
;
; tie combines:
; - symbol dereference
; - and write
; into a single operation.
;
; No eval needed.
; No guessing.
;
; Reading the code tells you immediately:
; "this writes through a symbol alias".
;
; Still runtime.
; Still call-by-reference.
; Just clearer.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

(set x:x 10)
(tie 'p 'x:x)           ; p becomes an alias (symbol-pointer) to x:x

(func (f p)
  (tie p 35))           ; implicit dereference + write

(f p)
(println x:x)           ;-> 35
